          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For trim, shelving and most cabinet work, 24 to 25 inches is plenty. To rip a 4x8 sheet of plywood down the middle you need more than 24 inches, so builders who break down a lot of sheet goods should look at saws with 30 inches or more, like the DEWALT DWE7491RS or Metabo HPT C10RJ."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a SawStop really worth the price?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For beginners, shared shops and anyone who values their fingers above the price difference, yes. The flesh-sensing brake typically turns blade contact into a minor cut. Activations destroy a brake cartridge and usually a blade, which you must replace, and it does not prevent kickback, so technique and guards still matter on a SawStop."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a table saw on a 15-amp household circuit?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, but it can trip the breaker under heavy load or if other things share the circuit. A dedicated 20-amp circuit is better. Use a 12-gauge extension cord, avoid power strips, and if you need a new circuit in a garage or shop, have a licensed electrician install it with the required permit."
          }
        },
