        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every jobsite table saw cuts wood. What separates the good ones is the fence, because a fence that does not stay parallel to the blade causes both inaccurate cuts and kickback. After that come rip capacity, the stand and, for some buyers, a flesh-sensing brake that turns a life-changing injury into a bandage. Price matters less than you would think once you get those right.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Jobsite Table Saw
          </h2>

          <p>
            A jobsite table saw is a compromise by design. It has to be light enough to carry or roll onto a site, but accurate enough to rip plywood and trim stock cleanly. The best ones get there by spending their money in the right places: a fence that locks parallel to the blade, a stiff table and arbor, and a stand that sets up quickly. The worst ones spend it on features that look good on the box and leave the fence as an afterthought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Almost every portable table saw in this class uses a 10-inch blade, a 15-amp universal motor and a no-load speed in the high thousands of rpm. That means the motors are broadly comparable, and power is rarely the deciding factor. A sharp, appropriate blade makes more difference to cut quality and to how hard the motor works than the difference between brands. A thin-kerf 24-tooth rip blade is the single best upgrade for ripping thick stock on a 15-amp saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            What separates them is the fence, the rip capacity, the stand, the dust collection and the safety system. Those are the sections of this guide, and they are the things to compare. A saw with a mediocre fence and a great stand is a worse tool than the reverse, because the fence determines whether every cut is square and whether the saw is likely to kick back.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are searching for a Delta 10 inch table saw, note that Delta’s current 10-inch saws, like the 36-725T2 contractor saw, are a different class of tool: heavier, with cast iron tables and larger footprints, intended to stay in a garage or shop. They are fine saws, but they are not designed to be loaded into a truck every morning. If the saw will never leave the shop, a contractor or cabinet saw may suit you better. If it will travel, stay in the jobsite category below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the rest of the cutting setup. A jobsite table saw handles rips and sheet goods; crosscuts on long boards are faster and safer on a miter saw. If you are buying both, our <a href="/best-miter-saw-stand/">miter saw stand guide</a> covers the stand side of that pairing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rip-fence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Rip Capacity and Fence Quality
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rip capacity is the maximum distance between the blade and the fence, and it decides what you can cut. The number many buyers fixate on is 24 inches, the width of half a 4x8 sheet of plywood. Because the blade removes a kerf, splitting a sheet exactly in half lengthwise needs a little more than 24 inches, and wider parts such as cabinet sides or 30-inch panels need more again. The DEWALT DWE7491RS lists 32-1/2 inches, the Metabo HPT C10RJ lists 35 inches, and the SawStop and Skil list 25-1/2 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            More capacity is not free. The fence rails have to extend further, which makes the saw longer or requires rails that telescope. That adds weight and moving parts. For most trim and cabinet work, 24 to 25 inches is plenty. For framers and builders who rip full sheets of subfloor or sheathing regularly, 30 inches or more saves cuts and a lot of awkward handling. Breaking down full sheets on a small saw is also a safety issue, which we cover below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fence quality matters more than capacity. A fence must lock exactly parallel to the blade, every time, without you checking both ends. If the far end of the fence toes in toward the blade, the stock pinches between the blade and the fence, and that is the classic setup for kickback. If it toes out, cuts come out tapered. Rack-and-pinion fences, which move both ends together on geared rails, are the best design for portable saws because they stay parallel through their full travel.
          </p>

          <p>
            DEWALT, Metabo HPT and Skil all use rack-and-pinion fences on the saws in this guide, and owners consistently rate them as the most accurate type in the category. Bosch and SawStop use different designs that are also well regarded. Avoid saws where the fence locks only at the front, unless the fence is very stiff; a front-locking fence on a light tube rail flexes under side pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the fence type, check it when the saw is new. Measure from the blade to the fence at the front and back of the blade with a combination square, and adjust the fence until the measurements match. Recheck after transport. Owners commonly report that a quick check every few weeks keeps a good fence true for years.
          </p>
        </div>

