              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best jobsite table saw for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The DEWALT DWE7491RS is the safest default. It combines a rack-and-pinion fence that stays parallel to the blade, a 32-1/2 inch rip capacity that handles full plywood sheets, a rolling stand and a full set of safety equipment. Buy the SawStop if safety is the priority, or the Bosch 4100XC-10 if you move the saw constantly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much rip capacity do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For trim, shelving and most cabinet work, 24 to 25 inches is plenty. To rip a 4x8 sheet of plywood down the middle you need more than 24 inches, so builders who break down a lot of sheet goods should look at saws with 30 inches or more, like the DEWALT DWE7491RS or Metabo HPT C10RJ.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a SawStop really worth the price?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For beginners, shared shops and anyone who values their fingers above the price difference, yes. The flesh-sensing brake typically turns blade contact into a minor cut. Activations destroy a brake cartridge and usually a blade, which you must replace, and it does not prevent kickback, so technique and guards still matter on a SawStop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a table saw on a 15-amp household circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but it can trip the breaker under heavy load or if other things share the circuit. A dedicated 20-amp circuit is better. Use a 12-gauge extension cord, avoid power strips, and if you need a new circuit in a garage or shop, have a licensed electrician install it with the required permit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for a table saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 15-amp table saw draws around 1,800 watts running and more at startup. A generator with at least 3,500 running watts and strong surge capacity is a sensible minimum for the saw alone; size up for vacuums and other tools. Soft-start saws help. Always run the generator outdoors, far from doors, windows and vents.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Delta 10 inch table saw a jobsite saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Delta’s current 10-inch saws, such as the 36-725T2, are contractor-style saws designed to stay in a shop. They are heavier with larger footprints and are not intended to be hauled to a site every day. For a saw that travels, choose a jobsite model like the DEWALT DWE7491RS or Bosch 4100XC-10.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 10-inch saw, or is an 8-1/4 inch compact enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An 8-1/4 inch saw cuts less deep, but still handles 2x lumber, trim, shelving and plywood. If portability matters more than maximum depth of cut, a compact saw like the DEWALT DWE7485 is easier to carry and set up. For thick hardwoods and the biggest rip jobs, choose a 10-inch saw.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
